---
/* The homepage headline: "Tousif Rahaman / Designs thoughtful products that
   drive results", from Figma's 20205:22325. Its entrance is CSS alone — see
   the keyframes below — and its exit is home-reel.ts, which fades it out as
   the cards rise.
   The two never touch the same element: the entrance animates `.hero__name`
   and `.hero__line` (`.hero__title` under reduced motion, where the reel
   never runs); the exit writes to `[data-reel-fade]` on `.hero__title`'s
   wrapper, because a running CSS animation outranks an inline style and
   would pin whatever the script tried to set.
   "thoughtful" additionally gets a one-shot decrypt/scramble reveal —
   `decrypted-text.ts`, wired up in index.astro's `<script>` alongside
   `home-reel.ts`. It starts scrambling immediately on load, running
   underneath this entrance rather than after it, and only ever rewrites
   `[data-decrypt-visual]`'s children, so it can't fight the fade/rise
   above. Skipped outright under reduced motion, where the plain word
   stands. */
---

<div class="hero" data-reel-hero>
	{/* data-nav-scrim-narrow: the stacked layout's nav-scrim target — see
	    SiteNav.astro. Below the reel's breakpoint the page is a plain scroll,
	    so the headline itself is what the nav watches. */}
	<div class="hero__frame" data-reel-fade>
		<h1 class="hero__title" data-nav-scrim-narrow>
			<span class="hero__name type-hero-name">Tousif Rahaman</span>
			{/* Broken where Figma breaks it. The frame is wide enough at 80px to
			    pull "products" up onto the first line, which would leave the
			    accent in the middle of a line rather than closing one. */}
			<span class="hero__line type-hero">Designs <em class="type-hero-accent" aria-label="thoughtful"><span data-decrypt-visual aria-hidden="true">thoughtful</span></em><br /> products that drive results</span>
		</h1>
	</div>
</div>

<style>
	/* Centred top to bottom in whatever box hosts it — the reel's pinned
	   viewport, or the stacked layout's first screen — and starting on the
	   nav's own left edge (SiteNav.astro's `.nav-shell`: gutter outside, a
	   --measure-chrome row centred inside), so the name sits on the same
	   line as the brand mark above it, as Figma draws them (both at x = 197
	   on a 1440 frame). */
	.hero {
		display: grid;
		align-content: center;
		padding-inline: var(--gutter);
		color: var(--text);
	}

	/* Only the left edge is the nav's. The right is left open to the
	   gutter: Montserrat at 80px sets "products that drive results" wider
	   than the nav's 1000px row (Figma's block is 1087 wide on a 1440
	   frame), and capping it there wraps "results" onto a line of its own.
	   The inset is the nav row's own centring margin, restated as padding
	   so the box itself still runs to the gutter. */
	.hero__frame {
		box-sizing: border-box;
		inline-size: 100%;
		padding-inline-start: max(0px, calc((100% - var(--measure-chrome)) / 2));
	}

	/* No measure cap: nothing sits beside the headline, and the frame above
	   already bounds it. The two lines are 16px apart, as drawn. */
	.hero__title {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: var(--spacing-xl);
		margin: 0;
		font: inherit;
	}

	.hero__name,
	.hero__line {
		display: block;
		color: var(--text);
	}

	/* `<em>` for the one word the sentence leans on; the class carries its
	   face. `font-style` is restated only so a browser default can't be read
	   as the reason it slants. */
	.hero__line em {
		font-style: italic;
	}

	/* The entrance — the name, then the sentence 80ms behind it, both rising
	   a little on --ease-out. Every keyframe ends on the element's own
	   resting style, so filling `backwards` is all that is needed, and once
	   each animation ends it leaves nothing behind. */
	@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
		.hero__name {
			animation: hero-arrive 600ms var(--ease-out) 200ms backwards;
		}

		.hero__line {
			animation: hero-arrive 600ms var(--ease-out) 280ms backwards;
		}
	}

	/* Reduced motion keeps the arrival and drops the movement: one short
	   fade of the finished headline. */
	@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
		.hero__title {
			animation: hero-fade 200ms ease backwards;
		}
	}

	@keyframes hero-arrive {
		from {
			opacity: 0;
			translate: 0 var(--spacing-md);
		}

		to {
			opacity: 1;
			translate: 0 0;
		}
	}

	@keyframes hero-fade {
		from {
			opacity: 0;
		}

		to {
			opacity: 1;
		}
	}
</style>
